  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Building a new house in Austin, Texas, means clearing a series of city requirements before and during construction: zoning, site and tree review, building and trade permits, utility service and a string of inspections. None of it is impossible, but Austin is a city where homework done early saves months later. Here is what to expect, in roughly the order you will run into it.</p>

<h2>1. Confirm your jurisdiction and zoning</h2>
<p>First, find out who actually governs your lot. Inside city limits, the <strong>City of Austin Development Services Department (DSD)</strong> handles residential review, permits and inspections. Just outside, you may be in Austin's extraterritorial jurisdiction, in another city like Round Rock or Pflugerville, or in unincorporated Travis, Williamson or Hays County, each with different rules.</p>
<p>If you are in the city, look up the zoning district for your lot. Zoning controls what you can build and how big:</p>
<ul>
<li>Allowed use and number of units (Austin has updated its residential rules in recent years, so check current allowances)</li>
<li>Front, side and rear setbacks</li>
<li>Maximum height and building coverage</li>
<li>Impervious cover limits, which cap how much of the lot can be roof, driveway and patio</li>
</ul>
<p>Also pull your deed restrictions and any HOA covenants. Those are private rules the city does not enforce, but your neighbors and HOA can. Location choice drives a lot of this, which is why we recommend reading <a href="/choosing-the-right-location-for-your-texas-dream-home/">choosing the right location for your Texas dream home</a> before you close on land.</p>

<h2>2. Get your site surveyed and studied</h2>
<p>Before design gets too far, collect the facts about your land:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Boundary and topographic survey</strong> showing lot lines, easements, slopes and existing structures.</li>
<li><strong>Tree survey</strong> if the lot has significant trees. Austin protects larger trees by trunk size, and removing or crowding them can require review, mitigation or a design change.</li>
<li><strong>Geotechnical (soil) report.</strong> Central Texas has a mix of expansive clay and shallow limestone, and your foundation engineer will want real data.</li>
<li><strong>Floodplain and watershed check.</strong> Some lots sit in floodplains or environmentally sensitive watersheds with extra limits.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Have your designer place the house around the trees and slopes from day one. Redesigning a footprint after the tree review comes back is one of the most common, and most avoidable, Austin delays.</p></div>

<h2>3. Prepare plans that meet code</h2>
<p>Austin adopts the International Residential Code and companion codes (electrical, plumbing, mechanical, energy) with local amendments. A typical residential submittal includes a site plan, floor plans, elevations, foundation and framing details, and energy compliance documents. Austin has a strong focus on energy efficiency, so expect your insulation, windows and HVAC to be checked against the local energy requirements. Confirm the current code editions on the city's website before your designer finalizes anything.</p>
<p>For a deeper look at the codes themselves, see <a href="/understanding-building-codes-in-austin-tx-what-you-need-to-know/">understanding building codes in Austin, TX</a>. If you are drawing early layouts yourself, a [[amz:graph_paper_notebook|graph paper notebook]] and a [[amz:laser_measure|laser measure]] make site walks and room sizing much faster.</p>

<h2>4. Apply for permits</h2>
<p>With plans ready, you apply through the city. Expect at least one round of reviewer comments. Answer every comment clearly, revise the drawings, and resubmit. Once the building permit is issued, the trade permits for electrical, plumbing and mechanical work are generally pulled by state-licensed contractors.</p>
<p>Fees and review times change, so check the current fee schedule and published review times rather than relying on a number a friend paid a few years ago. Build those figures into your <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">construction budget</a>.</p>

<h2>5. Plan utility connections early</h2>
<p>Utilities take longer than most first-time builders expect. Inside the city, Austin Energy provides electric service to most addresses and Austin Water handles water and wastewater, but some areas are served by other providers. Start the conversations early about:</p>
<ul>
<li>Water and wastewater taps and any impact or capital recovery fees</li>
<li>Electric service location, meter placement and temporary power for construction</li>
<li>Gas service if you want it</li>
<li>Septic permitting if the lot is not on sewer (usually handled through the county)</li>
</ul>

<h2>6. Pick a contractor (or decide to manage it yourself)</h2>
<p>Texas does not license residential general contractors at the state level, so vetting is on you. Ask for references, recent addresses you can drive by, proof of insurance and a written scope and schedule. If you plan to run the job yourself, read <a href="/how-to-be-your-own-general-contractor/">how to be your own general contractor</a> first. Planning tools help either way.</p>
<!--products:home_planner,books_contracting_guide,books_dummies|Planning resources for an Austin home build-->

<h2>7. Pass inspections from slab to final</h2>
<p>Once work starts, the city inspects at key stages: plumbing under the slab, foundation, framing and rough trades, insulation and energy, and finals. Do not cover work until it passes. When everything is signed off, the city issues the final approval you need to occupy the home. Keep a binder with every permit, inspection result and change order; your lender, insurer and future buyers will thank you.</p>

<h2>8. Budget time and money for the unknowns</h2>
<p>Between plan review, utility coordination and weather, Austin builds rarely run exactly to plan. Carry a contingency and keep communication tight with your designer, builder and lender. Our <a href="/new-home-construction-timeline/">new home construction timeline</a> shows where the usual slowdowns happen.</p>

<h2>Quick Austin build checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Zoning, deed restrictions and HOA rules reviewed</li>
<li>Boundary, topographic and tree surveys complete</li>
<li>Soil report in hand and foundation engineered</li>
<li>Plans and energy compliance ready for submittal</li>
<li>Utility providers contacted and fees estimated</li>
<li>Builder or subs selected, insurance in place</li>
<li>Permit issued and posted on site before work begins</li>
</ul>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Start with a zoning lookup and a call to the City of Austin Development Services Department to confirm current submittal requirements, code editions and fees. Then work through our <a href="/free-training/">free training</a> to map out your build from land to move-in.</p>
